    ],
    "cursorSync": "Off",
    "description": "NOAA GFS 10 m wind over South America for the next two days, read live from ERDDAP: WMO wind barbs on the model's own cells, and arrows over the flow field.",
    "editable": true,
    "elements": {
      "panel-1": {
        "kind": "Panel",
        "spec": {
          "data": {
            "kind": "QueryGroup",
            "spec": {
              "queries": [],
              "queryOptions": {},
              "transformations": []
            }
          },
          "description": "",
          "id": 1,
          "links": [],
          "title": "What you are looking at",
          "vizConfig": {
            "group": "text",
            "kind": "VizConfig",
            "spec": {
              "fieldConfig": {
                "defaults": {},
                "overrides": []
              },
              "options": {
                "code": {
                  "language": "plaintext",
                  "showLineNumbers": false,
                  "showMiniMap": false
                },
                "content": "**NOAA GFS 10 m wind** at 0.5°, read live from [PacIOOS ERDDAP](https://pae-paha.pacioos.hawaii.edu/erddap/griddap/ncep_global.html): one CSV request for `u` and `v` over the next two days, 2,703 cells from 40°S to 12°N every three hours, queried with DuckDB. No tile server and no preprocessing.\n\nBoth maps draw **`vectorfield`**, a kepler.gl layer type registered by [this plugin](https://github.com/ubica-ecuador/keplergl-grafana-panel). It takes the same U/V (or speed/direction) columns as its sibling `flowfield`, so changing a layer's type between the two keeps its columns and colour ramp.\n\n- **Top: WMO wind barbs on the data's own cells.** Half barb 5 kn, full barb 10, pennant 50, circle for calm. The feathers sit left of the staff in the northern hemisphere and right of it in the southern: look along the equator.\n- **Bottom: arrows on a screen grid**, sized and coloured by speed, over the flow field traced from the same rows. The grid is laid out in pixels, so the arrows stay legible at any zoom.\n\n**The layer panel on the left of either map is live**: expand a layer to switch *Symbol* (arrow, classified arrow, wind barb) and *Placement* (screen grid, data cells); with speed and direction columns instead of u and v it also offers *Direction is* (from or towards).\n\n**The bar at the bottom of either map walks the forecast**: it opens on the step nearest to now; drag its window or press play, and both maps move together.",
                "mode": "markdown"
              }
            },
            "version": "13.2.1"
          }
        }
      },
      "panel-2": {
        "kind": "Panel",
        "spec": {
          "data": {
            "kind": "QueryGroup",
            "spec": {
              "queries": [
                {
                  "kind": "PanelQuery",
                  "spec": {
                    "hidden": false,
                    "query": {
                      "datasource": {
                        "name": "duckdb"
                      },
                      "group": "motherduck-duckdb-datasource",
                      "kind": "DataQuery",
                      "spec": {
                        "format": 1,
                        "rawQuery": true,
                        "rawSql": "-- NOAA GFS 10 m wind, u and v, for the next two days in ONE request: 17\n-- three-hourly steps of 53 x 51 = 2,703 cells, 45,951 rows.\n--\n-- Every other cell of the 0.5 degree archive, so one per degree -- a regular\n-- lattice, which both velocity layers need: they deduce the spacing from the\n-- coordinates and draw nothing if it wobbles.\n--\n-- Every step comes back, each row with its time, and the map's clock -- the bar\n-- at the bottom -- chooses which one is drawn: the latest inside its window.\n-- The dashboard's range, now-90m to now+90m, is that window when the map opens,\n-- so the step on show is the one nearest to now.\n--\n-- The request starts at the step at or before now - 90 minutes, so the window\n-- lies wholly inside the data. Started any later, kepler would clip the\n-- window's leading edge to the first step and leave it narrower than the three\n-- hours between steps, and a window that narrow, playing, spends stretches with\n-- no step inside it and draws nothing.\n--\n-- The window is written into the URL from `now()` itself, not from a variable:\n-- it stays the same URL for three hours, and no hidden variable rides along in\n-- shared links. `{0}` and `{1}` are `format`'s, not Grafana's.\n--\n-- The URL goes to ERDDAP through the gateway's cache (`feeds` on this compose\n-- network): ERDDAP is asked once per three-hour window and every visitor after\n-- the first is answered from the cache, HEAD included. ERDDAP builds each answer\n-- on the spot, and a miss can take longer than a dashboard query is allowed.\n--\n-- `.csv0` has no header and no units row, so read_csv names by position. Only\n-- `[` and `]` need escaping (%5B/%5D). The two subscripts must be written\n-- identically or ERDDAP answers 400.\n--\n-- On a miss the cost is DuckDB's, twice over: it sends a HEAD before the GET,\n-- and ERDDAP builds the whole response for both. `SET force_download` would halve it, but it\n-- outlives this query on a connection every dashboard shares, and would stop\n-- range requests into large Parquet elsewhere; `http_get` cannot send this URL\n-- at all -- it rewrites the query string, and ERDDAP answers 400.\nSELECT column0       AS time,\n       column1       AS latitude,\n       column2 - 360 AS longitude,  -- ERDDAP publishes longitude as 0..360\n       -- `u` and `v` are the names the velocity layers look for.\n       column3       AS u,\n       column4       AS v\nFROM read_csv(\n  format('http://feeds/erddap/griddap/ncep_global.csv0?ugrd10m%5B({0}):1:({1})%5D%5B(-40.0):2:(12.0)%5D%5B(260.0):2:(310.0)%5D,vgrd10m%5B({0}):1:({1})%5D%5B(-40.0):2:(12.0)%5D%5B(260.0):2:(310.0)%5D',\n    strftime(time_bucket(INTERVAL 3 HOUR, (now() AT TIME ZONE 'UTC') - INTERVAL 90 MINUTE),\n             '%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ'),\n    strftime(time_bucket(INTERVAL 3 HOUR, (now() AT TIME ZONE 'UTC') - INTERVAL 90 MINUTE) + INTERVAL 48 HOUR,\n             '%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ')),\n  header = false,\n  columns = {'column0': 'TIMESTAMP', 'column1': 'DOUBLE', 'column2': 'DOUBLE',\n             'column3': 'DOUBLE', 'column4': 'DOUBLE'},\n  nullstr = 'NaN')\nWHERE column3 IS NOT NULL AND column4 IS NOT NULL\n"
                      },
                      "version": "v0"
                    },
                    "refId": "A"
                  }
                }
              ],
              "queryOptions": {},
              "transformations": []
            }
          },
